TypeScript is a statically-typed superset of JavaScript that provides additional type annotations and features to enhance code readability, maintainability, and safety. It adds type definitions to the language, enabling developers to catch errors at compile-time rather than runtime. TypeScript compiles to JavaScript, allowing developers to use it seamlessly with existing JavaScript libraries and frameworks.

Duck Typing is a design pattern in object-oriented programming that focuses on an object's capabilities rather than its class inheritance hierarchy. In other words, an object is considered to be of a specific type if it exhibits certain behaviors or methods, regardless of its class or ancestry. This means that if an object has the same interface (i.e., methods and properties) as another object, it can be used interchangeably, even if they belong to different classes.

TypeScript embraces Duck Typing by allowing developers to define interfaces that describe the shape of an object, including its properties and methods. When a variable is declared as an interface type, TypeScript checks that the object assigned to it conforms to that interface. This ensures that objects are used correctly and that their expected behavior is enforced. 

For example, consider the following TypeScript code:

```typescript
interface Shape {
  draw(): void;
}

class Circle implements Shape {
  draw(): void {
    console.log('Drawing a Circle');
  }
}

class Square implements Shape {
  draw(): void {
    console.log('Drawing a Square');
  }
}

function drawShape(shape: Shape) {
  shape.draw();
}

drawShape(new Circle()); // Output: Drawing a Circle
drawShape(new Square()); // Output: Drawing a Square
```

In this example, we define an interface `Shape` that specifies a single method `draw`. We then create two classes `Circle` and `Square` that implement this interface and provide their own implementations of the `draw` method. The `drawShape` function takes a parameter of type `Shape`, which allows it to accept any object that conforms to the `Shape` interface. 

Duck Typing enables us to use objects of different classes interchangeably as long as they exhibit the same interface. In this example, we can pass instances of either `Circle` or `Square` to `drawShape` because they both implement the `Shape` interface and provide the `draw` method. 

TypeScript combines the benefits of Duck Typing with its strong typing system, allowing developers to write more robust and maintainable code. By defining interfaces, TypeScript enables the use of Duck Typing at compile-time, catching errors early and improving the overall development experience.

To create a Discord bot using Discord.js v14 that has a `ban` command as a slash command, follow these steps:

1. Create a new Discord application in the Discord Developer Portal and add a new Bot to it.

2. Generate a Bot Token for the application and keep it secure.

3. Install the latest version of Discord.js and its dependencies using npm:

   ```
   npm install discord.js dotenv
   ```

4. Create a `.env` file in the root directory of your project and add the following content:

   ```
   TOKEN=<your_bot_token_here>
   ```

5. Create a new directory called `commands` in the root directory of your project.

6. Create a new JavaScript file called `ban.js` inside the `commands` directory.

7. In the `ban.js` file, add the following code:

   ```javascript
   const { SlashCommandBuilder } = require('discord.js');

   module.exports = {
     data: new SlashCommandBuilder()
       .setName('ban')
       .setDescription('Bans a user from the server.')
       .addUserOption(option =>
         option
           .setName('user')
           .setDescription('The user to ban.')
           .setRequired(true),
       ),
     async execute(interaction) {
       const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');

       // Your ban logic here
       // For example:
       await interaction.guild.members.ban(user);

       await interaction.reply({
         content: `${user.tag} has been banned.`,
         ephemeral: true,
       });
     },
   };
   ```

   This code defines a slash command called `/ban` with a required user option. It also includes placeholder logic to ban the user and send an ephemeral message confirming the ban.

8. In the root directory of your project, create a new file called `index.js`.

9. In the `index.js` file, add the following code:

   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits } = require('discord.js');
   const { TOKEN } = require('./config');

   const client = new Client({
     intents: [
       GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
       GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
     ],
   });

   require('./commands/ban')(client);

   client.login(TOKEN);
   ```

   This code sets up a Discord client instance with the necessary intents, imports the `ban` command from the `commands` directory, and logs in the bot with the Bot Token stored in the `.env` file.

10. Create a new file called `.env` in the root directory of your project, and add your Discord Bot Token as follows:

   ```
   TOKEN=<your_bot_token_here>
   ```

11. Run your Discord bot by running `node index.js` in your terminal.

Your Discord bot is now ready to receive `/ban` slash commands from users in your Discord server! Note that the bot needs the `BanMembers` permission to actually ban users. You can grant this permission by navigating to your Discord server's settings > Roles > @everyone > Permissions and checking the "Ban Members" box.

Here's a possible implementation of a C++ function that converts Markdown to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <regex>

// Markdown to HTML conversion table
static const std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> MARKDOWN_TO_HTML {
    {"#", "<h1>"},
    {"##", "<h2>"},
    {"###", "<h3>"},
    {"####", "<h4>"},
    {"#####", "<h5>"},
    {"######", "<h6>"},
    {"**", "<strong>"},
    {"__", "<strong>"},
    {"*", "<em>"},
    {"_", "<em>"},
    {"``", "<pre><code>"},
    {"[", "<a href=\""],
    {"]", "\" title=\""],
    {"`", "'"},
    {"\\", "\\\\"},
    {">", "<blockquote>"}
};

// Helper function to escape special characters in HTML
static std::string escape_html(const std::string& str) {
    std::string result;
    for (char c : str) {
        switch (c) {
            case '&':
                result += "&amp;";
                break;
            case '<':
                result += "&lt;";
                break;
            case '>':
                result += "&gt;";
                break;
            case '"':
                result += "&quot;";
                break;
            default:
                result += c;
                break;
        }
    }
    return result;
}

// Convert a Markdown string to HTML
std::string markdown_to_html(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::vector<std::string> lines = split_string(markdown, '\n');

    // Loop through each line
    for (const auto& line : lines) {
        std::string current_html;

        // Check for block-level elements
        if (line.starts_with("#")) {
            std::string header_level = line.substr(0, line.find_first_of(' '));
            std::string header_text = line.substr(header_level.length() + 1);
            current_html += "<h" + MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at(header_level) + ">" + escape_html(header_text) + "</h" + header_level[0] + '>';
        } else if (line.starts_with("``")) {
            std::string code_text = line.substr(3, line.size() - 4);
            current_html += MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at("``") + escape_html(code_text) + "</code></pre>";
        } else if (line.starts_with("[") && line.find_last_of(']') != std::string::npos && line.find_first_of(' ', 1) != std::string::npos) {
            std::string link_text = line.substr(line.find_last_of(']') + 1);
            std::string link_url = line.substr(line.find_first_of(' ', 1) + 1, line.find_last_of('"') - (line.find_first_of(' ', 1) + 1));
            current_html += MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at("[") + link_url + MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at("]") + ">" + escape_html(link_text) + "</a>";
        } else if (line.starts_with(">")) {
            std::string blockquote_text = line.substr(1);
            current_html += MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at(">") + "<p>" + escape_html(blockquote_text) + "</p>";
        } else {
            std::vector<std::string> inline_tokens = split_string(line, {"**", "__", "*", "_", "`"});
            std::string plain_text = inline_tokens.back();
            inline_tokens.pop_back();

            // Apply inline formatting
            for (const auto& token : inline_tokens) {
                if (token == "**" || token == "__") {
                    current_html += "<strong>" + escape_html(plain_text) + "</strong>";
                    break;
                }
                if (token == "*" || token == "_") {
                    current_html += "<em>" + escape_html(plain_text) + "</em>";
                    break;
                }
                if (token == "`") {
                    current_html += MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at("``") + escape_html(plain_text) + "</code></pre>";
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (current_html.empty()) {
                current_html += "<p>" + escape_html(plain_text) + "</p>";
            }
        }

        // Append the resulting HTML for this line
        if (!html.empty()) {
            html += "\n";
        }
        html += current_html;
    }

    return html;
}

// Split a string into a vector of substrings using a delimiter
std::vector<std::string> split_string(const std::string& str, char delimiter) {
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    std::string token;
    for (char c : str) {
        if (c == delimiter) {
            if (!token.empty()) {
                result.push_back(token);
                token.clear();
            }
        } else {
            token += c;
        }
    }
    if (!token.empty()) {
        result.push_back(token);
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "## Hello, world! \n"
                           "This is a **bold** and an _italic_ text. \n"
                           "Here's a link to [Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/). \n"
                           "> This is a blockquote. \n"
                           "```
                           This is a code block.
                           ```";

    std::string html = markdown_to_html(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This implementation uses an unordered map to translate Markdown syntax to HTML tags. The `escape_html()` function escapes special characters in HTML to prevent XSS attacks. The `split_string()` function splits a string into a vector of substrings using a delimiter. 

The main `markdown_to_html()` function first splits the input string into lines, then loops through each line and applies the appropriate formatting based on the first character(s). It also handles nested inline formatting (bold, italic, code) and inline links. Block-level elements (headers, code blocks, blockquotes) are recognized based on the first character(s) of the line.

Note that this implementation is not a comprehensive Markdown parser and does not support all of the features of the Markdown specification. It's just a simple example that demonstrates how to convert basic Markdown to HTML using C++ and the standard library.

