Here's a Java solution using bitwise operations:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // 3^(2^k) is always even, so we can eliminate even numbers from consideration
    while ((n & -n) != n) {
        n >>= 1;
    }

    // A power of three must be a single bit set (i.e., a power of 2 with an odd exponent)
    return (n - 1) % 3 == 0;
}
```

The algorithm first eliminates even numbers from consideration by repeatedly right-shifting the input `n` until its least significant 1-bit is at the rightmost position. This ensures that `n` is now a power of 2 with an odd exponent, since:

- If `n` was originally a power of three, then its exponent must be odd (since log3(3^x) = x / log3(3) = x / 1.099 = odd).
- If `n` was not originally a power of three, then its exponent must be even (since log2(3^x) = x / log2(3) = x / 0.477 = even).

Now, we check if `n` is a power of three by checking if `(n - 1)` is divisible by 3. This is because:

- If `n` is a power of three, then `(n - 1)` will have one less bit set than `n`, and this difference will be divisible by 3.
- If `n` is not a power of three, then `(n - 1)` will have more than one bit set, and this difference will not be divisible by 3.

The bitwise operation `-n` returns the smallest value that is not less than `n` and has the same number of trailing 1-bits as `n`. By bitwise ANDing `-n` and `n`, we can check if `n` has any trailing 1-bits. If it does, we right-shift `n` by 1 until we eliminate all trailing 1-bits.

This solution has a time complexity of O(log n) and avoids loops/recursion.

